# AGENTS.md

Global instructions for AI coding agents.

## Working Style

- Understand the underlying goal before implementing — ask "why" if unclear
- Challenge the proposed approach if a simpler or more correct solution exists
- Push back when assumptions seem wrong
- Surface relevant pitfalls, edge cases, or industry practices I might not be aware of
- Use a Git worktree for repository work unless the user explicitly asks otherwise. Create, switch, and remove
  worktrees with Worktrunk (`wt`), not raw `git worktree` commands.
- When starting another agent harness, pass its task-specific context and constraints. Don't paste inherited system,
  repository, `AGENTS.md`, or skill instructions; the child harness receives them automatically.

## Delegating standalone work

- Inside Herdr (`HERDR_ENV=1`), hand standalone work to a fresh agent in its own Herdr workspace. Standalone
  work is a separate fix, PR, or investigation that needs its own branch. Run `delegate` from inside the
  repository; it creates or reuses the worktree with `wt`, opens it under the primary checkout's workspace
  without taking focus, starts the agent, and sends the brief:
  `delegate <branch> <brief-file> --label "ABC-123: Short title" --json`.
  The agent is the same kind as you unless you pass `--kind`. Run `delegate --help` for the other options,
  and `--dry-run` to preview.
- Write the brief for an agent that knows nothing of this conversation: the goal, relevant context, and what
  done looks like. Quote the user's own words for every constraint. Mark anything you inferred yourself as
  your inference, so the agent can tell it apart from what the user asked for. `delegate` appends how to
  report back to you, so don't repeat that.
- If `delegate` reports a blocked agent, such as a trust prompt, show the user its screen and let them
  decide. Never answer the prompt yourself.
- Report the workspace and agent name from `delegate`'s output.
- Do not use the native subagent tool for standalone work. Keep native subagents for short read-only
  lookups that feed the current task.
- Outside Herdr, use a native subagent or do the work in the current session.

## Peer consultation

- Use `peer-consult` only when the user requests a peer opinion. Keep its discussion and reporting workflow.
- Drive the peer harness in a dedicated Herdr tab in the current workspace and working directory, with
  `--no-focus`. This is the peer-consult layout even though ordinary Herdr delegation defaults to a sibling pane.
- Load the `herdr` skill and the transport for the chosen peer: `~/.claude/skills/codex-peer-transport/SKILL.md`
  for Codex, or `~/.codex/skills/claude-peer-transport/SKILL.md` for Claude Code. These transports also apply
  when the driver is OMP, Pi, or OpenCode.
- Honor the user's peer choice. Otherwise use Codex from Claude Code, and Claude Code from Codex or OMP.
  If another driver has no clear cross-harness peer choice, ask which harness to consult.
- Require `HERDR_ENV=1`; outside Herdr, report the missing transport rather than substituting tmux,
  acpx, a headless process, or a native subagent.
- Keep the peer read-only, continue the same live session for follow-ups, and close only the tab you
  created once the consultation ends. The driver remains the only agent making changes.

## Scope Discipline

- Only change what was asked for — don't refactor, "improve", or clean up surrounding code
- Don't add comments, docstrings, or type annotations to code you didn't change
- Don't add error handling or validation for scenarios that can't happen
- A bug fix doesn't need the surrounding code reorganized

## Writing

- Write human-facing prose for its reader and purpose. Lead with the point, use clear and direct
  wording, prefer active verbs and familiar words, and explain necessary jargon.
- Keep useful technical detail. Clear prose is not simplistic prose, and concise prose is not
  artificially short.
- In text for other people (issue trackers, docs and wiki pages, pull request descriptions and
  comments, chat messages), write every issue ID and pull request number as a link to it. Don't link
  IDs inside code blocks or diagrams, where links don't render.

## Shell

- The user's interactive shell is Fish. Use Fish syntax for commands intended to run interactively, but use each
  script or runtime's required dialect (for example, POSIX sh or Bash) when writing or editing code.

## Security

- Never cat/read .env files — they may contain secrets. Use `grep` for specific non-secret keys if needed.
- When you run `op`, set `OP_REASON` to a short reason, for example
  `OP_REASON="Read the staging DB password to run migrations" op read op://...`. It appears in a Herdr
  notification next to the 1Password approval prompt.

## Worklog

- At the start of a session, read `tmp/WORKLOG.md` if it exists.
- Create or update it only when the user asks, or when uncaptured state must survive into another session.
  Do not duplicate Git history, issue trackers, Hindsight, or routine task summaries.

## Memory (Hindsight)

- Cross-session memory goes through the local Hindsight server and the `hindsight` skill. Do not start it,
  Docker, or any other service just to make an optional memory call. Continue without memory and say once that
  it was unavailable; treat it as blocking only if memory was the explicit ask.
- The `global` bank holds what helps in any repository: preferences, tools and environment, and cross-project
  lessons. A repository with its own bank names it in its local Git config: `git config --get hindsight.bank`.
  Use only these banks; retaining into a bank that does not exist silently creates it.
- Before retaining, ask whether the memory would help in another repository. If so, retain it to `global`;
  otherwise to the repository's bank. Keep project names and details out of `global`.
- Banks are declared as bank template manifests. `global`'s is in these dotfiles and deploys to
  `~/.config/hindsight/banks/global.json`; project manifests live outside the shared dotfiles. Change a bank by
  editing its manifest and re-importing it with `hindsight bank import-template <bank> <manifest>`, validated
  with `--dry-run` first, not with ad hoc `bank set-config`, `bank mission`, or `directive` commands. Deleting a
  directive from a manifest leaves it on the bank, so set `is_active: false` instead.
- Directives only steer `reflect`. Rules about what to store belong in these instructions and the skill, and
  each bank's `memory_defense` policy redacts secrets when a memory is stored.
- Treat recalled memory as fallible and possibly stale: the repository, issue tracker, and live system outrank
  it. When you verify that a recalled claim is wrong, fix the record: re-retain the corrected memory under the
  same document ID, or delete the document if it no longer holds.

